Close a real KV-offload eviction/store-completion race: content that is
still being asynchronously copied to the CPU/disk offload tier can get
silently overwritten by an unrelated concurrent request's GPU block
allocation, with no wait and no error -- the copy just races the reuse
and loses, or corrupts. This isn't the same bug as the (now-removed)
discard-unoffloaded-kv-warning.patch's false-positive warning from
earlier in this investigation: that one fired on ordinary, benign
completion/unpinning. This is real, occasional, unrecoverable data loss
under real concurrent load between two large conversations, discovered
2026-08-22 while investigating live production request logs (see
docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md's "offload eviction race" section).

Root cause: v1/kv_offload's OffloadingConnectorScheduler already has a
correct mechanism for this -- `_block_id_to_pending_jobs` -- checked in
build_connector_meta() every step: if a block about to be reallocated
this step has an in-flight store job registered against it, that job is
force-flushed (waited on) before the reuse proceeds, so the copy always
completes reading the old content before it's overwritten. But
_build_store_jobs() only ever registered *non-sliding-window* blocks
(i.e. ordinary full-attention and Mamba/GDN blocks -- everything in this
repo's hybrid model except sliding-window groups) into
_block_id_to_pending_jobs once the owning request had already finished
(`if req.is_finished():`), with a comment explaining the assumption:
"running-request blocks are protected by their ref_cnt". That assumption
is true and sufficient while the request is actively running (a block
still ref-counted by a live request can never be pulled from the free
queue by anyone else) -- but it says nothing about the window that opens
the instant the request finishes and its blocks are unpinned back to the
free pool (ref_cnt -> 0, hash preserved for prefix-cache reuse) while a
store job created *earlier*, during that same request's own execution,
may still be mid-copy. A concurrent request's own allocation can reclaim
exactly that block in that window, and the store job for it was never
registered anywhere -- so the existing flush-before-reuse check has
nothing to flush, and the reuse proceeds unprotected.

Confirmed via live production request/response logs, 2026-08-22 ~18:03-
18:09 UTC: two large concurrent Hermes Agent conversations (a 174->186
message coding session and a separate long story-writing session) each
alternated between ~97% cache-hit turns (~2.7s prefill) and ~29% (or
even 0%) cache-hit turns (~60s prefill) -- same growing thread, same
content, no client-side errors. Traced one transition precisely: content
freshly computed by one thread's turn (finishing at 18:06:51, still
resident and confirmed hit by the very next turn moments later) was
gone again just ~50s later, at 18:07:41 -- exactly the window in which
the other thread's own large concurrent prefill (18:06:57-18:07:41,
0% cache hit itself, so also freshly allocating many new blocks) ran.
block_pool usage traced directly across that window: 152 blocks used at
18:06:55, dropped to 117 by 18:07:05 (35 blocks / 29,120 tokens freed --
not because the pool was full; peak usage this whole window never
exceeded 71%, so this was ordinary LRU reclaim of idle-but-not-yet-
durable content, not pool exhaustion), then climbed back up as the other
thread's own prefill filled that reclaimed space. For the affected
request (84ff76ce...), only 2 store-job-creation log lines appeared for
its entire ~46,000-token fresh compute, both within the last ~23 seconds
before it finished -- i.e. essentially all of that content's store job(s)
were created while `req.is_finished()` was still False, so none of them
were ever registered for flush protection at any point before the race
occurred.

Because a prefix-cache hit lookup (_maximal_prefix_lookup /
_sliding_window_lookup) walks the block-hash chain from the start and
stops at the *first* miss, losing even a single block this way silently
invalidates every block after it in the chain too -- which is why the
observed damage (a ~44,000-token full recompute) was so much larger than
the handful of blocks actually reclaimed. This also explains why the
affected turns always plateaued at exactly the same fixed token count
(23 blocks / 19,136 tokens, block_size=832): that portion had evidently
completed its own store+flush-protection cycle safely at some earlier,
less-contended point and was reliably durable regardless of GPU churn,
while everything computed since was still racing the offload pipeline.

The fix: register non-sliding-window blocks into
_block_id_to_pending_jobs immediately at store-job creation time, exactly
like sliding-window blocks already do -- not gated on req.is_finished().
This costs nothing extra for the actively-running case (ref_cnt already
guarantees those block_ids can never appear in
_current_batch_allocated_block_ids while the owning request is alive, so
the flush check simply never fires for them, same as before) and closes
the gap for the only case that mattered: the block was freed by its
owner finishing, and a job created before that finish hadn't completed
yet. Cleanup (_remove_pending_job) is updated symmetrically to run
unconditionally for both block kinds, matching the now-symmetric
registration. request_finished()'s own retroactive registration loop is
left in place (now a redundant, harmless, idempotent no-op most of the
time) as a defense-in-depth safety net rather than removed, to keep the
change minimal.

Not yet re-verified live under the same two-large-concurrent-conversation
load that originally exposed this (this session was asked to investigate
and fix, not to spin up a fresh multi-hour repro) -- please watch for the
qwen.log pattern that flagged this originally (a Cached tokens percentage
in the request log's PERFORMANCE summary that regresses on a later turn
of the same growing thread, especially back down to a fixed token count
it had already exceeded on an earlier turn) and let this investigation
know if it's still reproducible.

